## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Pattern Classification

| Original Code | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on abort | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `Thread.Abort()` in `Stop()` | Force termination | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue or exit the loop naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, the thread is truly stuck.
        // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in modern .NET.
        // Options: log a warning, or move uncooperative work to a child process.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

```csharp
// Before: may block indefinitely with no way to interrupt
private Item GetNextItem() { ... }

// After: must accept and honor the token
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
    return _queue.Take(token);

    // If using Thread.Sleep to poll:
    // Replace Thread.Sleep(ms) with:
    token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(millisecondsTimeout);
}
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---|---|
| `GetNextItem()` blocks without checking the token | `Cancel()` has no effect until the blocking call returns — cancellation stalls indefinitely |
| Not passing token through the full call chain | Same problem — cancellation only works at checkpoints that inspect the token |
| Expecting `Cancel()` to act like `Abort()` | Cooperative cancellation is **not immediate** — it only fires at the next `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or token-aware API call |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal timers and registered callbacks |
| Replacing `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in `Stop()` with the same pattern | Use `_workerThread.Join(timeout)` instead — it actually waits for the thread to exit |

### If `GetNextItem` Truly Cannot Be Modified

If the blocking call is in code you don't control (third-party, native), move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` as the last-resort equivalent of `Thread.Abort()`. There is no in-process alternative in modern .NET.
